C2. 
1. What happened and how I found it

We received a support issue related to file imports. As part of the investigation, I had to validate 12 file imports. Out of those, 10 were successful, while 2 files required further investigation.
The impact was that those two files could not be confirmed as successfully imported, which affected the expected data-import process.

2. Root cause
I investigated the issue by validating the import scenarios and checking the behavior of the affected files. I worked with Pramod, the Development Lead, to investigate the two unsuccessful imports.
hrough this investigation, we identified the underlying issue and shared the findings with the external team for further action.I would explain the exact technical root cause based on the confirmed development findings rather than make assumptions.

3. Explaining it to the client
I communicated the findings to the relevant stakeholders and development team as part of the support-ticket investigation.
I explained that 10 out of 12 files were imported successfully, while 2 needed further investigation. I made the status clear so that the team understood which imports were working and which ones still required attention.

4. Stopping it from happening again
From the QA side, I focused on validating the import scenarios and tracking the unsuccessful files separately.

I would also ensure that:
- All 12 files are included in the regression test coverage.
- Successful and unsuccessful import scenarios are validated.
- Error messages and import results are verified.
- The two affected files are retested after the external team provides the fix.
This helps prevent the same issue from going unnoticed in future releases.

5. Looking back

One thing I would improve is preparing a more comprehensive file-import validation checklist upfront, covering different file types and import conditions.

I would also ensure that the root cause, affected files, test evidence, and retest results are documented clearly in the support ticket.
